<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#national-redoubt-switzerland">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Redoubt (Switzerland)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Réduit national</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Redoubt was the Swiss defence plan announced by General Henri Guisan in July 1940, after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> almost surrounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a>. The army was to give up the populated lowlands and hold a fortified Alpine core, centred on the fortresses of Saint-Maurice, the Gotthard and Sargans, making an invasion costly and denying the vital Alpine passes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-redoubt-switzerland.jpg" alt="National Redoubt (Switzerland)" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Auge=mit · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reduit_Schweiz_Neu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The idea of a fortified Alpine refuge was older than the war. Fortification of the Swiss Alps began in the 1880s, soon after the Gotthard railway opened, with forts in the style of the Belgian engineer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-alexis-brialmont">Henri Alexis Brialmont</a> at Airolo and the Oberalp, Furka and Grimsel passes, and works cut into the mountainsides around Saint-Maurice. Interest waned after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, but in the 1930s, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> built the Maginot Line and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> its border fortifications, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> re-examined its defences, helped by the wish for public works during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>. Design began in 1935 and construction in 1937.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The plan of General Henri Guisan called for delaying actions in the broken border country so that the army could withdraw in good order to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, where the government could hold out for a long time. Sargans needed new works, because the draining of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> wetlands had opened an easy approach. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the border zones lost priority on 23 June 1940, and the final plan of 12 July provided for a retreat to the Alps with supplies for indefinite resistance.</p><p>The plan was made known at the Rütli Report, a meeting of the army staff and the whole officer corps at the founding site of the Swiss Confederation. The lowlands, with most of the people and industry, were in effect to be given up, and the gold of the Swiss National Bank was moved from Zürich towards the Gotthard and Bern. After the Germans swiftly overran the Balkan countries in April 1941 the whole army was mobilised.</p><p>The strategy was one of deterrence, backed by economic concessions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Germany's invasion plan, Operation Tannenbaum, foresaw the division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Field Marshal Montgomery judged the redoubt an impractical absurdity, since it could not shield civilians from reprisals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> the redoubt lost priority: from 1953 Swiss policy stressed defence of the borders and cities and extended defence in depth to the whole country. The forts, holding guns, dormitories, kitchens and hospitals for 100 to 600 soldiers, were mostly closed in the 1990s and early 2000s as weapons changed. Several, such as Sasso da Pigna and Faulensee, became museums, and others now serve as data centres, a hotel or stores for cheese and valuables.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National Redoubt (Switzerland)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In both world wars the army mobilised under an elected general, Ulrich Wille in 1914 and Henri Guisan in 1939; in 1940 Guisan announced the strategy of the <b>National Redoubt</b>, a defence of the Alpine core.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Military</a></li></ul></section>
